\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A trace ratio separates two weighted cycles}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{The alternating cycles at #{t=1} and #{t=2} remain different even
under the [broader Laplacian comparison](fdip-000G)
#{L'=cPLP^T}, with #{c>0} and #{P} a vertex permutation.
To prove this, write #{\operatorname{tr}M=\sum_iM_{ii}} for the
trace of a square matrix and define
##{R(L)=\frac{\operatorname{tr}(L^2)}{(\operatorname{tr}L)^2}}
for a nonzero real symmetric positive semidefinite #{L} on a counting
space. Its eigenvalues are nonnegative and at least one is positive,
so #{\operatorname{tr}L>0}.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.5.2}{boyd2004convex}, pp. 646-647, gives the symmetric
spectral decomposition and trace formula used here.
The [positive graph energy](fdip-0008) supplies the semidefinite
hypothesis for a weighted Laplacian. The restriction #{L\ne0} is
essential: incidence data with only loops have #{L=0}, where this ratio
is undefined.}

\p{The ratio is unchanged by the declared comparison. For square real
matrices, finite sums give
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{tr}(MN)
 &=\sum_i\sum_jM_{ij}N_{ji}\\
 &=\sum_j\sum_iN_{ji}M_{ij}
  =\operatorname{tr}(NM).
\end{aligned}}
Thus #{\operatorname{tr}(PMP^T)=\operatorname{tr}M}. If
#{L'=cPLP^T}, then #{(L')^2=c^2PL^2P^T}, and
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{tr}L'&=c\operatorname{tr}L,\\
\operatorname{tr}((L')^2)&=c^2\operatorname{tr}(L^2).
\end{aligned}}
The factors #{c^2} cancel from the ratio, proving #{R(L')=R(L)}.
Hence #{R} is a well-defined invariant of these equivalence classes.
It uses the counting-space operator, not the matrix of a bilinear form
after an arbitrary nonorthogonal change of basis.}

\p{For the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003), #{t>0}, the
[orthonormal spectral basis](fdip-0037) gives eigenvalues
#{0,2,2t,2+2t}, including at #{t=1}. Consequently
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{tr}L_t&=4(1+t),\\
\operatorname{tr}(L_t^2)
 &=4+4t^2+4(1+t)^2\\
 &=8(t^2+t+1),\\
R(t):=R(L_t)&=\frac{t^2+t+1}{2(t+1)^2}.
\end{aligned}}
In particular
##{\begin{aligned}
R(1)&=\frac38,\qquad R(2)=\frac7{18},\\
R(2)-R(1)&=\frac1{72}>0.
\end{aligned}}
There can be no #{c>0} and vertex permutation #{P} with
#{L_2=cPL_1P^T}. Thus the associated finite Gaussian backgrounds
cannot be exactly relabeled into each other either. This calculation
separates the stated pair; it does not claim a classification of all
backgrounds.}

\p{The reciprocal identity #{R(t)=R(1/t)} is consistent with this
comparison. A one-step rotation #{p(i)=i+1\pmod4} exchanges the
alternating edge weights #{1,t} and leaves the common diagonal
#{1+t} unchanged. Its coordinate permutation therefore satisfies
##{PL_tP^T=tL_{1/t}.}
Since #{1/t>0}, the reciprocal Laplacians are equivalent under
#{\approx}. Their equal ratios do not demonstrate a failure of
completeness.}

\p{A fixed-mass calculation shows what changes in the induced state.
Set #{m=1} and retain the same labeled vertices #{R=\{0,2\}}.
The [alternate-vertex elimination](fdip-000F) has #{s=t+2}, giving
##{\begin{aligned}
\alpha(t)&=s-\frac{1+t^2}{s}=\frac{4t+3}{t+2},\\
\beta(t)&=\frac{2t}{t+2},\\
S_t&=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha(t)&-\beta(t)\\
                         -\beta(t)&\alpha(t)\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Hence the effective coupling changes from #{\beta(1)=2/3} to
#{\beta(2)=1}, and the two precisions are
##{\begin{aligned}
S_1&=\begin{pmatrix}7/3&-2/3\\-2/3&7/3\end{pmatrix},\\
S_2&=\begin{pmatrix}11/4&-1\\-1&11/4\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Their determinants are #{5} and #{105/16}. The off-diagonal entry
of #{S_t^{-1}} is #{\beta(t)/\det S_t}. By the
[marginal expectation identity](fdip-000E), the same labeled observable
therefore has different normalized expectations:
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle\phi_0\phi_2\rangle_{t=1,m=1}&=\frac{2}{15},\\
\langle\phi_0\phi_2\rangle_{t=2,m=1}&=\frac{16}{105}.
\end{aligned}}
This comparison fixes the mass, coordinates, reference pairing and
partition. The labeled observable is not itself asserted invariant under
every relabeling; the trace ratio already established the broader
inequivalence.}

\p{Scale invariance of #{R} does not mean equality of fixed-mass
states. On an eigenvector of #{L} with eigenvalue #{\lambda>0},
replacing #{L} by #{cL} changes the covariance eigenvalue from
#{1/(\lambda+m^2)} to #{1/(c\lambda+m^2)} when #{c\ne1}.
The exact finite differences above do not construct a physical map
from an environment to the parameter #{t}.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.trace_mul_comm} and
\leanref{Matrix.trace_smul} supply the cyclic trace and scalar identities.
The nonzero domain, ratio cancellation and four-cycle values are the
additional explicit calculations above.}
